::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Which tool is used to transfer the length of a segment during a construction?
{
~A. Protractor#A protractor measures angles, not segment lengths.
=B. Compass#Correct. A compass holds a fixed opening to transfer a segment length.
~C. Calculator#A calculator does not transfer geometric distances.
~D. Notebook margin#A margin is not a construction tool.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
If segment CD is constructed as a copy of segment AB, which statement should be true?
{
~A. CD > AB#A copied segment should not be longer than the original.
~B. CD < AB#A copied segment should not be shorter than the original.
~C. C \= D#Points are locations, not segment lengths.
=D. CD \= AB#Correct. A copied segment has the same length as the original.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
When copying angle ABC, where is the first compass arc centered?
{
=A. At B#Correct. B is the vertex of angle ABC.
~B. At A#A is on a side of the angle, not the vertex.
~C. At C#C is on a side of the angle, not the vertex.
~D. At any point by sight#The arc must be centered at the angle vertex.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Which action would make a copied segment unreliable?
{
~A. Drawing a ray from the new starting point#A starting ray is part of the construction.
~B. Placing the compass point on the new endpoint#This is needed to transfer the length.
=C. Changing the compass opening before marking the endpoint#Correct. Changing the opening changes the transferred length.
~D. Marking the arc intersection on the ray#This marks the copied segment endpoint.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
If angle DEF is a copy of angle ABC, which statement should be true?
{
~A. mangle DEF > mangle ABC#A copied angle should not be larger than the original.
=B. mangle DEF \= mangle ABC#Correct. A copied angle has the same measure as the original.
~C. mangle DEF < mangle ABC#A copied angle should not be smaller than the original.
~D. DE \= AB#Copying an angle does not require these segment lengths to match.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Why is estimating by sight not enough when copying an angle?
{
=A. Appearance alone does not prove the angle measure was preserved#Correct. Arcs and compass distances provide construction evidence.
~B. Sight estimation always creates equal angle measures#Sight estimation does not guarantee equality.
~C. Angle copies never require tools#This standard requires geometric tools.
~D. A copied angle must be larger than the original#A copy should match the original angle measure.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
What does a segment bisector do?
{
~A. Copies a segment to a new ray#That describes segment-copy construction.
~B. Measures an angle in degrees#A segment bisector works with segment length.
~C. Renames the endpoints#Naming endpoints does not bisect a segment.
=D. Divides a segment into two equal parts#Correct. A segment bisector creates two congruent segment parts.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
If M is the midpoint of segment AB, which statement is true?
{
~A. AM > MB#A midpoint creates equal parts.
~B. AB \= MB#The whole segment is not equal to one part.
=C. AM \= MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two equal lengths.
~D. A \= B#Distinct endpoints are not equal segment lengths.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
What must be true about a perpendicular bisector of AB?
{
=A. It crosses AB at its midpoint and forms right angles#Correct. Both midpoint and perpendicular relationships are required.
~B. It only crosses endpoint A#A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at its midpoint.
~C. It copies AB onto a new ray#That is a segment-copy construction.
~D. It changes the length of AB#The original segment length does not change.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
If ray BD bisects angle ABC, which statement is true?
{
~A. mangle ABD > mangle DBC#An angle bisector creates equal angle measures.
~B. AB \= BC#Angle bisection does not require equal side lengths.
~C. BD \= AB#Ray length is not the angle-bisector conclusion.
=D. mangle ABD \= mangle DBC#Correct. The ray divides the angle into two congruent angles.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
Why are matching compass openings important when constructing a bisector?
{
~A. They make the drawing darker#The purpose is accuracy, not appearance.
=B. They provide evidence of equal distances#Correct. Equal compass openings support equal-distance relationships.
~C. They remove the need for a straightedge#A straightedge is still used to draw the final line or ray.
~D. They measure every angle in degrees#A compass transfers distance, not degree measure.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
Which evidence best supports that line line l is the perpendicular bisector of AB at M?
{
~A. Line line l looks centered on AB#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
~B. Line line l is drawn longer than AB#Line length does not prove perpendicular bisection.
=C. AM \= MB and line line l forms right angles with AB#Correct. A perpendicular bisector requires both conditions.
~D. A and B are labeled#Labels do not prove the required relationships.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
In If p, then q, what is p called?
{
~A. The conclusion#The conclusion is q.
~B. The converse#The converse is If q, then p.
~C. The contrapositive#The contrapositive is If not q, then not p.
=D. The hypothesis#Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the converse of If p, then q?
{
=A. If q, then p#Correct. The converse switches p and q.
~B. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
~C. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If p, then not q#This is not the converse.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the inverse of If p, then q?
{
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse.
~B. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
=C. If not p, then not q#Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~D. If p, then q#This is the original conditional.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the contrapositive of If p, then q?
{
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse.
=B. If not q, then not p#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~C. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
~D. If p, then not q#This only negates q.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does if and only if mean?
{
~A. Only the conditional direction is included#A biconditional includes both directions.
~B. Only the inverse direction is included#The inverse is not the same as a biconditional.
=C. Both the conditional and converse directions are included#Correct. If and only if means both directions hold.
~D. Both statement parts are negated#Negation does not create a biconditional.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which if-then statement matches All rectangles have four right angles?
{
=A. If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles#Correct. All rectangles means every rectangle has the property.
~B. If a figure has four right angles, then it is a rectangle#This is the converse.
~C. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it does not have four right angles#This is the inverse.
~D. If a figure does not have four right angles, then it is not a rectangle#This is the contrapositive.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
For If a figure is a square, then it is a rectangle, which statement is the contrapositive?
{
~A. If a figure is a rectangle, then it is a square#This is the converse.
~B. If a figure is not a square, then it is not a rectangle#This is the inverse.
~C. If a figure is a square, then it is not a rectangle#This negates only the conclusion.
=D. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it is not a square#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does not do in a related conditional statement?
{
~A. It switches the order of p and q#Switching order creates a converse.
=B. It negates a statement part#Correct. Not denies the condition or conclusion.
~C. It creates an if and only if statement#A biconditional is different from negation.
~D. It removes the conclusion#The conclusion can still exist in negated form.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If angle 1 and angle 2 are vertical angles, which statement is supported?
{
~A. mangle 1 + mangle 2 \= 180°#Vertical angles are congruent, not always supplementary.
=B. angle 1 ≅ angle 2#Correct. Vertical angles are congruent.
~C. angle 1 and angle 2 form a linear pair#Vertical angles are not adjacent linear-pair angles.
~D. mangle 1 \= 90°#Vertical angles are not always right angles.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If angle A and angle B form a linear pair, which statement is true?
{
~A. mangle A \= mangle B#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
~B. mangle A + mangle B \= 90°#This is a right-angle sum, not a linear-pair sum.
~C. angle A and angle B are vertical angles#A linear pair is adjacent.
=D. mangle A + mangle B \= 180°#Correct. Linear-pair angles are supplementary.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If two angles are supplementary, what is true about their measures?
{
=A. They add to 180°#Correct. Supplementary angles have measures that add to 180°.
~B. They add to 90°#Complementary angles add to 90°.
~C. They are always congruent#Supplementary angles are not always congruent.
~D. They are always vertical angles#Supplementary angles do not have to be vertical.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If two angles form a linear pair and one angle measures 70°, what is the measure of the other angle?
{
~A. 70°#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
~B. 120°#This does not add to 180° with 70°.
=C. 110°#Correct. 180° - 70° \= 110°.
~D. 20°#This does not add to 180° with 70°.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
Which reason supports mangle A + mangle B \= 180° when angle A and angle B are supplementary?
{
~A. Vertical angles are congruent#This does not explain supplementary angle measures.
=B. Definition of supplementary angles#Correct. Supplementary angles add to 180° by definition.
~C. Definition of midpoint#Midpoint applies to segments.
~D. The angles look adjacent#Appearance is not the definition.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q26::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
A proof states two lines are parallel because corresponding angles are congruent. What type of evidence is being used?
{
=A. A line and angle relationship#Correct. Corresponding angle congruence can support parallel-line reasoning.
~B. Appearance-based evidence#The reason is a line-angle relationship.
~C. A measurement guess#The statement is not based on guessing.
~D. A counterexample#The statement supports a conclusion, not disproves one.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q27::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
What is the purpose of reasons in a proof?
{
~A. To make the proof longer#Reasons justify, not lengthen, a proof.
~B. To replace all given information#Given information remains part of the proof.
~C. To avoid mathematical vocabulary#Proof reasons require precise vocabulary.
=D. To justify why each statement follows#Correct. Reasons connect statements to definitions, properties, or theorems.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q28::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
Which pair best describes a basic proof setup?
{
~A. Guess and drawing#Proofs require reasoning, not guessing.
~B. Opinion and example#Proof is not based on opinion.
=C. Given and prove#Correct. A proof often begins with given information and a statement to prove.
~D. Color and shape#Visual features alone do not define a proof setup.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q29::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
What is the first step in a proof by contradiction?
{
=A. Assume the opposite of what you want to prove#Correct. Contradiction proof begins with the negation of the desired conclusion.
~B. State the conclusion without support#Proof requires support.
~C. Find one example that looks correct#Contradiction proof does not begin with one visual example.
~D. Erase the original claim#The original claim remains the goal.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q30::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
In a proof by contradiction, what does an impossible result show?
{
~A. The original statement must be rejected#The impossible result rejects the opposite assumption.
~B. The proof has become a counterexample#Contradiction proof is not the same as a counterexample.
~C. The diagram should be ignored#The reasoning, not ignoring diagrams, drives the conclusion.
=D. The opposite assumption cannot be true#Correct. This supports the original statement.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q31::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
Which proof step is incomplete?
{
~A. Statement: angle 1 and angle 2 are vertical angles. Reason: Given.#This statement has a valid reason.
=B. Statement: angle 1 ≅ angle 2. Reason: It looks true.#Correct. Appearance is not a valid proof reason.
~C. Statement: angle 1 ≅ angle 2. Reason: Vertical angles are congruent.#This statement has a valid reason.
~D. Statement: mangle A + mangle B \= 180°. Reason: Definition of supplementary angles.#This statement has a valid reason.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q32::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
Which statement best describes a valid geometric proof?
{
~A. A drawing that appears correct#Appearance alone is not proof.
~B. A list of conclusions with no reasons#A proof must justify conclusions.
=C. A logical argument with statements supported by reasons#Correct. Valid proofs connect each statement to a reason.
~D. A single example that matches the claim#A single example does not prove a universal statement.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q33::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
What is a counterexample?
{
~A. A second example that supports a claim#A counterexample disproves rather than supports.
~B. A drawing that looks accurate#Appearance is not the defining feature.
~C. A proof with no reasons#A proof needs reasons.
=D. One example that shows a statement is false#Correct. One valid counterexample disproves a universal claim.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q34::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Which counterexample disproves the claim All rectangles are squares?
{
=A. A rectangle with side lengths 3 and 8#Correct. It is a rectangle but not a square.
~B. A square with side length 6#A square does not disprove the claim.
~C. A triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5#A triangle is not a rectangle.
~D. A circle with diameter 8#A circle is not a rectangle.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q35::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Which counterexample disproves the claim All acute angles are congruent?
{
~A. Two 45° angles#These are congruent and do not disprove the claim.
~B. A 90° angle and a 45° angle#A 90° angle is not acute.
=C. A 30° angle and a 50° angle#Correct. Both are acute, but they are not congruent.
~D. Two right angles#Right angles are not acute.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q36::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Why is the argument The segments are congruent because they look equal invalid?
{
~A. Congruent segments cannot appear in diagrams#Congruent segments can appear in diagrams.
=B. It uses appearance instead of a valid reason#Correct. A valid argument needs evidence such as markings, measurements, or a proven relationship.
~C. Segments never need evidence#Segment congruence requires evidence.
~D. The conclusion is always false#The conclusion may be true, but the reason is invalid.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q37::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Why is one example not enough to prove a universal claim?
{
~A. One example can only disprove statements#One example can support exploration, but not prove every case.
~B. Universal claims are never true#Some universal claims are true when proven.
=C. A universal claim must work for every case#Correct. Universal claims require reasoning that covers all cases.
~D. Counterexamples only work with numbers#Counterexamples can use geometric figures.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q38::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Which argument is valid?
{
=A. The angles are congruent because they are vertical angles#Correct. The conclusion is supported by a known angle relationship.
~B. The lines are parallel because they look parallel#Appearance alone is not valid evidence.
~C. The angle is right because it seems close to 90°#Approximate appearance is not proof.
~D. The segments are equal because they are drawn darkly#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q39::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
Which explanation best supports the conclusion angle 1 ≅ angle 2?
{
~A. They appear to have the same measure#Appearance alone is not proof.
~B. They are near each other in the diagram#Location alone does not prove congruence.
~C. They are both drawn with dark lines#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
=D. They are vertical angles, and vertical angles are congruent#Correct. This gives evidence and a valid reason.
}
::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q40::
Question ID: GEO_U01_A_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If two angles form a linear pair and one angle measures 65°, what is the measure of the other angle?
{
~A. 65°#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
=B. 115°#Correct. 180° - 65° \= 115°.
~C. 125°#This does not make a sum of 180° with 65°.
~D. 25°#This does not make a sum of 180° with 65°.
}
